 best captures the evidence.", "watch": "Any shift in the national political environment or a late-breaking scandal could move a race this tight either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Susan Collins (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Maine's Senate race is one of the tightest in the country, with four-term Republican incumbent Susan Collins defending her seat against Democrat Troy Jackson. Polling in late September and early October shows the race essentially tied, with averages giving Jackson a lead of roughly one to two points. Some surveys show Collins ahead, others show Jackson up four, and a CBS/YouGov poll found the candidates dead even.\n\nMarkets price Jackson at about a 59 percent chance of winning, and several expert raters call the race a Democratic lean while others call it a pure tossup. Collins holds a large fundraising edge and remains personally popular, but Maine's Democratic lean and a favorable national environment for Democrats cut against her. Negative stories have touched both campaigns, including Jackson's apology over remarks about Jewish Mainers and Democratic attacks on Collins. The race remains genuinely competitive heading into the final stretch.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7341, "margin": 3.16}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6906, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.626, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6915, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5751, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6364, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6474, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6668, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.6495, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.6328, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.6994, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.6388, "margin": 1.5}]}